WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.200
Data stack extras.

2
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.573
These are the modern data stack names that orbit the pipelines you built.

3
00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.800
Let's go through them.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.667
Polars is a Rust-powered DataFrame library.

5
00:00:11.967 --> 00:00:16.567
It's lazy and multicore, and often ten times faster than pandas or more.

6
00:00:16.867 --> 00:00:22.700
It's increasingly the default for heavy tabular work, and pandas knowledge transfers in about a day.

7
00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.458
DuckDB is an in-process analytical SQL engine.

8
00:00:27.458 --> 00:00:29.933
Think SQLite, but for analytics.

9
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:36.367
You query parquet or CSV files with full SQL, no server at all, and it's absurdly fast.

10
00:00:36.667 --> 00:00:40.200
That makes it a favorite for local data work and pipeline glue.

11
00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.300
dbt is the transform layer in ELT.

12
00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:49.600
You write SQL models with refs, tests, and docs, all version-controlled like code.

13
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.300
Analytics engineering exists as a job title because of dbt.

14
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:57.667
It all lives in the repo, reviewed like any other code.

15
00:00:58.867 --> 00:01:02.000
Dagster and Prefect are the modern rivals to Airflow.

16
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:05.700
Dagster is asset-centric, with strong typing and testing.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.581
Prefect is the lightweight, pythonic one.

18
00:01:08.581 --> 00:01:11.100
Concepts from Airflow transfer directly.

19
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.300
A feature store is a central registry of features.

20
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.567
It serves the same feature values to training, offline, and to inference, online.

21
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:22.921
That kills training serving skew.

22
00:01:22.921 --> 00:01:26.967
Feast is the open-source standard, and Tecton is the managed one.

23
00:01:27.267 --> 00:01:28.967
Same values, both worlds.

24
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:33.967
Lakehouse table formats add database features to lake files.

25
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:38.100
They add ACID transactions and time travel on top of the files.

26
00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:41.967
The result is warehouse reliability at lake economics.

27
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:47.191
Delta versus Iceberg was the twenty twenty-three to twenty twenty-five turf war.

28
00:01:47.191 --> 00:01:52.300
Since Databricks bought Tabular the two are converging, so expect both in one shop.

29
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.300
Great Expectations is data-quality testing.

30
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:01.700
You declare expectations, like this column is never null, or values stay in range.

31
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.984
They run inside your pipelines and fail loudly.

32
00:02:04.984 --> 00:02:07.333
Think of them as unit tests for data.

33
00:02:08.533 --> 00:02:13.600
Shadow deployment runs the new model on live traffic, but throws its outputs away.

34
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.667
You compare it against the incumbent, risk-free, before an A B test.

35
00:02:17.967 --> 00:02:21.533
Together with canary releases, it's the safe-rollout toolkit.

36
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:25.333
Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes.

37
00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:29.933
Charts template your deployments and services, driven by values files.

38
00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:33.005
So installing our whole stack is one command.

39
00:02:33.005 --> 00:02:37.500
It's assumed knowledge wherever Kubernetes shows up in a job description.

40
00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:42.833
Prometheus and Grafana are the standard open-source monitoring pair.

41
00:02:43.133 --> 00:02:45.695
Prometheus scrapes and stores the metrics.

42
00:02:45.695 --> 00:02:48.500
Grafana turns them into dashboards and alerts.

43
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:51.767
This is the concrete implementation of monitoring.

44
00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:56.533
gRPC is Google's binary RPC protocol.

45
00:02:56.833 --> 00:03:00.373
It runs over HTTP 2 with typed protobuf contracts,

46
00:03:00.373 --> 00:03:04.833
which makes it faster and stricter than REST for service calls.

47
00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:09.167
Triton and most serving stacks expose it alongside HTTP.

48
00:03:10.367 --> 00:03:11.281
Let's recap.

49
00:03:11.281 --> 00:03:14.633
Polars and DuckDB make local data work fast.

50
00:03:14.933 --> 00:03:18.667
dbt, Dagster, and Prefect transform and orchestrate.

51
00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:23.367
Feature stores and Great Expectations keep data consistent and tested.

52
00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:28.533
And Helm, Prometheus, Grafana, and gRPC deploy and monitor the stack.
